Here is a simple L3 interview-style answer:

Role of QEMU
QEMU is the actual virtualization engine that runs the virtual machine.

It emulates CPU, memory, disk, network, and devices.

KVM uses QEMU to create and run VMs.

KVM provides hardware acceleration, while QEMU provides emulation.

Simple line:
QEMU runs the VM and emulates hardware devices.

Role of libvirt
libvirt is a management layer for virtualization.

It provides APIs and services to manage VMs.

It communicates with KVM/QEMU.

Used for starting, stopping, migration, snapshots, networking, and storage management.

Simple line:
libvirt is the management service that controls KVM and QEMU.

Role of virt-manager
Virtual Machine Manager (virt-manager) is a graphical GUI tool.

Used to create and manage VMs visually.

Useful for administrators who prefer GUI instead of commands.

Simple line:
virt-manager is a GUI tool for managing KVM virtual machines.

Role of virsh
virsh is the command-line interface for libvirt.

Used to manage VMs from terminal.

Can start, stop, reboot, migrate, and edit VMs.

Example:
virsh list
virsh start vm1
virsh shutdown vm1
Simple line:
virsh is the CLI command tool used to manage VMs through libvirt.

One-Line Overall Summary
QEMU → Runs the VM

KVM → Gives hardware acceleration

libvirt → Management layer

virsh → CLI management tool

virt-manager → GUI management tool


